mere:
For even the finest; and the public’s right,
A tree’s mere firewood, unless humanised;
Which well the Greeks knew, when they stirred the bark

mermaids:
And visited the sea;
The mermaids in the basement
Came out to look at me,

merciful:
Behold!—the world of books is still the world;
And worldlings in it are less merciful
And more puissant. For the wicked there

merciless:
I huddle them like sheep;
Merciless shepherd-dog am I
And shepherd-watch I keep.

merriest:
Because her words had fretted me;
Not warbling quite her merriest tune
Bird-like from tree to tree.

merlasses:
Look at dat bib--you's ez du'ty ez me.
Look at dat mouf--dat's merlasses, I bet;
Come hyeah, Maria, an' wipe off his han's.

merchantmen:
Strews the landing with opal bales;
Merchantmen poise upon horizons,
Dip, and vanish with fairy sails.
